Inside this `.filter` callback, `value.toLowerCase()` does not depend on the iterator variable `policy`, so it is recomputed for every policy in the collection. Since `Object.values(policies ?? {})` can span every workspace, this adds O(n) redundant work that produces the same lowercase string on each iteration.

Hoist the lowercased value out of the loop:

```ts
const lowerCasedValue = value.toLowerCase();
const matches = Object.values(policies ?? {}).filter((policy) => policy?.name?.toLowerCase() === lowerCasedValue);
```

Inside the `findLast` predicate, `decodeURIComponent(feed)` does not depend on the iterator variable `route` — `feed` is the outer function parameter, so this call recomputes the exact same value on every iteration of the navigation-stack scan. Iterator-independent calls inside array-method callbacks should be hoisted out to avoid the redundant O(n) work.

Hoist the decoded feed once before the loop and reuse it:

```tsx
const decodedFeed = decodeURIComponent(feed);
const detailsRoute = settingsNavigatorState.routes.findLast((route) => {
    // ...
    return routeCardID === cardID && decodeURIComponent(routeFeed) === decodedFeed;
});
```

`getIsOffline()` is invoked inside the `.filter` callback but does not use the `policy` iterator variable — it reads module-level network state and returns the same value on every iteration. This makes it an iterator-independent call that runs redundantly O(n) times over the active policies, scaling with workspace count.

Hoist the call outside the `.filter` so it executes once:

```ts
const isOffline = getIsOffline();
const activePolicies = getActivePolicies(policies, currentUserLogin).filter(
    (policy) => shouldShowPolicy(policy, isOffline, currentUserLogin) && canMemberWrite(policy, currentUserLogin, CONST.POLICY.POLICY_FEATURE.WORKFLOWS_PAYMENTS),
);
```

Inside the `.filter((key) => ...)` callback, `policyID.value?.map((policyID) => \`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_TAGS}${policyID}\`)` rebuilds the entire mapped key array on every iteration over `Object.keys(allPolicyTagLists)`. The mapped array is independent of the iterator variable `key`, so it recomputes the same result each pass — redundant O(n·m) work.

Hoist the mapped key set out of the loop and reuse it inside the filter:

```tsx
const selectedTagKeys = new Set(policyID.value?.map((id) => `${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_TAGS}${id}`));
const selectedPoliciesTagLists = Object.keys(allPolicyTagLists ?? {})
    .filter((key) => {
        const isSelected = selectedTagKeys.has(key);
        return policyID.isNegated ? !isSelected : isSelected;
    })
    ?.map((key) => getTagNamesFromTagsLists(allPolicyTagLists?.[key] ?? {}))
    .flat();
```

Inside the `.filter((key) => ...)` callback, `policyID.value?.map((policyID) => \`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES}${policyID}\`)` rebuilds the entire mapped key array on every iteration over `Object.keys(allPolicyCategories)`. That mapped array does not depend on the iterator variable `key`, so it produces the same result every time — O(n·m) work that scales with both the policy-categories collection and the selected policy IDs.

Hoist the mapped set out of the loop so it is computed once, then check membership inside the filter:

```tsx
const selectedCategoryKeys = new Set(policyID.value?.map((id) => `${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_CATEGORIES}${id}`));
const selectedPoliciesCategories: PolicyCategory[] = Object.keys(allPolicyCategories ?? {})
    .filter((key) => {
        const isSelected = selectedCategoryKeys.has(key);
        return policyID.isNegated ? !isSelected : isSelected;
    })
    .map((key) => Object.values(allPolicyCategories?.[key] ?? {}))
    .flat();
```

`Object.values(CONST.SEARCH.RECEIPT_TYPE)` is called inside the `.filter()` callback but does not depend on the `receiptType` iterator variable, so it rebuilds the same array on every iteration. Iterator-independent calls inside array methods produce redundant O(n) work that should be hoisted out of the loop.

Hoist the lookup once (ideally as a `Set` for O(1) membership) before iterating:

```tsx
const validReceiptTypes = new Set<string>(Object.values(CONST.SEARCH.RECEIPT_TYPE));
const initiallySelectedItems = searchAdvancedFiltersForm?.receiptType
    ?.filter((receiptType) => validReceiptTypes.has(receiptType))
    .map((receiptType) => {
        const receiptTypeName = translate(getReceiptTypeTranslationKey(receiptType as ValueOf<typeof CONST.SEARCH.RECEIPT_TYPE>));
        return {name: receiptTypeName, value: receiptType};
    });
```

`getAllPolicyExpenseChatReportActions(allReports, allReportActions)` is invoked inside the `for (const item of selectedOptions)` loop, but its arguments (`allReports`, `allReportActions`) are invariant across iterations. The function iterates over every report on each loop pass, producing an identical result each time — O(reports × selectedOptions) redundant work. This partly undoes the PR's own goal of removing the per-render filtering by re-introducing per-iteration filtering.

Hoist the call out of the loop so the filtered map is computed once:

```ts
const activeRoute = Navigation.getActiveRoute();
const searchData = searchResults?.data;
const policyExpenseChatReportActions = getAllPolicyExpenseChatReportActions(allReports, allReportActions);

for (const item of selectedOptions) {
    // ...
    const invite = moveIOUReportToPolicyAndInviteSubmitter(
        itemReport,
        adminPolicy,
        formatPhoneNumber,
        policyExpenseChatReportActions,
        // ...
    );
}
```

`CONST.REGEX.EXPENSIFY_POLICY_DOMAIN_NAME` is a getter that allocates a new `RegExp` object on every access (see `src/CONST/index.ts` line 4974). Calling it inside the `for...of` loop means a new regex is constructed per iteration, even though the pattern is iterator-independent -- only the `.test(login.partnerUserID)` argument depends on the loop variable.

Hoist the regex before the loop:

```ts
const policyDomainRegex = CONST.REGEX.EXPENSIFY_POLICY_DOMAIN_NAME;
for (const login of Object.values(logins)) {
    // ...
    if (policyDomainRegex.test(login.partnerUserID)) {
        continue;
    }
    // ...
}
```

`getMoneyRequestParticipantsFromReport(selfDMReport, currentUserPersonalDetails.accountID).map(...)` is called inside the `for (const draftTransaction of transactions)` loop but does not depend on `draftTransaction`. The result is identical on every iteration, creating redundant O(n) computation.

Hoist the `selfDMParticipants` computation above the loop:

```ts
const selfDMParticipants = getMoneyRequestParticipantsFromReport(selfDMReport, currentUserPersonalDetails.accountID).map((participant) => ({
    ...participant,
    iouType: CONST.IOU.TYPE.TRACK,
}));
for (const draftTransaction of transactions) {
    setMoneyRequestParticipants(draftTransaction.transactionID, selfDMParticipants);
    setTransactionReport(draftTransaction.transactionID, {reportID: CONST.REPORT.UNREPORTED_REPORT_ID}, true);
}
```

`isIOUReport(report)` does not depend on the iterator variable `item` and is called on every iteration of the `.filter()` callback, producing the same result each time. Note that the same call also exists at line 133 in the pre-existing code. Both should be hoisted.

Hoist the call before the `.filter()`:
```ts
const isIOU = isIOUReport(report);
return Object.values(transactions || {}).filter((item) => {
    const isUnreported = isUnreportedTransaction(item);
    if (isIOU && !isUnreported) {
        return false;
    }
    // ... and later:
    // if (isIOU && transactionAmount === 0) {
```

`getSortedTagKeys(optimisticTagLists)` is called inside the nested `for` loop over transactions, but it does not depend on any iterator variable (`report` or `transaction`). The same value is already computed outside the loop (around line 2164) for the `perLevelSingleTag` computation. Since `optimisticTagLists` is constant across all iterations, this redundantly recomputes the sorted keys for every transaction in every report.

Reuse the `sortedTagKeys` variable that is already computed in the `perLevelSingleTag` block. Hoist it to the same scope level (alongside `perLevelSingleTag`) and reference it in both places:

```ts
// Compute once, outside the loop (already exists for perLevelSingleTag)
const sortedTagKeys = tagListKeys.length > 1 ? getSortedTagKeys(optimisticTagLists) : [];

// Then inside the multi-level tag auto-select block, remove:
// const sortedTagKeys = getSortedTagKeys(optimisticTagLists);
// and use the outer `sortedTagKeys` directly.
```

`[styles.alignItemsCenter]` and `[styles.mh4, styles.pv2, styles.productTrainingTooltipWrapper]` create new array instances on every iteration of the `.map()` call, but neither depends on the iterator variables (`key`, `item`, or `index`). These allocations are redundant and scale with the number of saved searches.

Hoist both style arrays above the `.map()` call:

```tsx
const itemStyle = [styles.alignItemsCenter];
const tooltipWrapperStyle = [styles.mh4, styles.pv2, styles.productTrainingTooltipWrapper];

const savedSearchesMenuItems = savedSearches
    ? Object.entries(savedSearches).map(([key, item], index) =>
          buildSavedSearchMenuItem({
              ...
              itemStyle,
              tooltipWrapperStyle,
          }),
      )
    : [];
```

`participantsLength` is computed inside the `.reduce()` callback but does not depend on the iterator variable `splitShareAccountID`. The expression `newAccountIDs.includes(accountID) ? newAccountIDs.length - 1 : newAccountIDs.length` produces the same result on every iteration.

Hoist it above the `.reduce()` call:
```tsx
const participantsLength = newAccountIDs.includes(accountID)
    ? newAccountIDs.length - 1
    : newAccountIDs.length;

const updatedSplitShares = accountIDs.reduce((acc, splitShareAccountID) => {
    // ... use participantsLength directly
}, {});
```
This applies to both duplicated blocks (in `updateCurrency` at ~line 630 and `handleAmountChange` at ~line 688).

