The `participantsPolicyTags` computation via `.reduce()` is inside the `for (const [index, item] of transactions.entries())` loop, but it does not depend on the loop variable (`item` or `index`). It only depends on `selectedParticipants` and `allPolicyTags`, which are loop-invariant. This means the same result is recomputed on every iteration, creating O(n * m) overhead where n is the number of transactions and m is the number of participants.

Hoist the computation above the `for` loop:

```tsx
const participantsPolicyTags = selectedParticipants.reduce<Record<string, PolicyTagLists>>((acc, participant) => {
    if (participant.policyID) {
        acc[participant.policyID] = allPolicyTags?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY_TAGS}${participant.policyID}`] ?? {};
    }
    return acc;
}, {});

for (const [index, item] of transactions.entries()) {
    // ... use participantsPolicyTags inside the loop ...
}
```

