The `Date.now()` call inside the `.some()` callback is iterator-independent - it produces the same timestamp regardless of which entry is being processed. This creates O(n) overhead.

**Fix**: Hoist the timestamp call outside the loop:

```typescript
// Skip duplicates (same loopCount + same reasoning text)
const isDuplicate = state.entries.some((entry) => entry.loopCount === data.loopCount && entry.reasoning === data.reasoning);

if (!isDuplicate) {
    const timestamp = Date.now();  // Hoist outside
    state.entries.push({
        reasoning: data.reasoning,
        loopCount: data.loopCount,
        timestamp,
    });
    store.set(reportID, state);
    notifyListeners(reportID, state.entries);
}
```

